course: english
lesson: 51
title: SATs: the reading paper
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: SATs are national tests that Year 6 children in England sit in May; the reading paper gives you about three texts, one hour and 50 marks.
explain: The texts usually get harder as you go, so the last one may feel "well long" (very long), but reading it in chunks and keeping calm gets you through.
explain: Many marks are lost not from not knowing but from not doing exactly what the question says: "find and copy one word" means one word, and "tick two" means two ticks.
explain: The marks show how much to write: a 2-mark "Explain two ways…" needs two points, and many questions want evidence, a quote or a detail from the text.
explain: Picture Ethan muttering "This question is sus" ("sus" means suspicious, a bit dodgy): the teacher says "Read it again", because a calm second reading usually shows what is being asked.
fact: In England, Year 6 children (aged 10 to 11) take national tests called SATs in May.
fact: The reading paper usually has three texts, lasts one hour and is worth 50 marks.
fact: The texts usually get harder through the paper.
fact: "Find and copy one word" means copying exactly one word from the text; writing a whole sentence can lose the mark.
fact: "Tick two" means exactly two ticks; a third tick loses the mark.
fact: The number of marks often shows how many points are needed: "Explain two ways" needs two.
fact: Underlining the key words in a question helps you see exactly what is asked.
fact: Many questions need evidence from the text: a quote or a clear detail, not just your opinion.
fact: You may skip a hard question and come back to it if there is time.
fact: The questions are not designed to trick you, but they must be read carefully.
fact: Staying calm helps you think; panic does not sharpen your focus.
fact: SATs do not decide whether you go to secondary school: everyone moves up, and the results mostly measure how much progress children have made.
fact: In Glasgow "hen" is a fond word for a girl or woman, and "dinnae" means "don't"; in Singapore English "can or not?" means "is it allowed?"
exercise: Who sits the SATs in England? | Year 6 children, in May | Every child, every single week | Only the children who want to | Teachers, before the summer holidays
exercise: How long is the SATs reading paper? | One hour | Ten minutes | Three hours | The whole school day
exercise: About how many texts and marks are in the reading paper? | Three texts, about 50 marks | One text, about 10 marks | Ten texts, about 500 marks | Two long texts, about 100 marks
exercise: The question says "Find and copy one word". Mia copies a whole sentence. What happens? | She may lose the mark | She gets extra marks for it | Nothing, because more is always better | The marker picks out the right word for her
exercise: The question says "Tick two". Josh ticks three. What happens? | He loses that mark | He gets a bonus mark for trying | The marker chooses two of his three | Nothing at all happens
exercise: Why underline the key words in a question? | To see exactly what is asked | To make the page look neat | Because markers count the lines | To use up the spare minutes
exercise: "Explain two ways the boy shows he is brave." Ella gives one. What is wrong? | The question asks for two | One way is always plenty | She should have given five | Nothing; her answer is perfect
exercise: What does "use evidence from the text" mean? | Back up your answer with a quote or detail | Say what you think, without using the text | Copy out the whole page, word for word | Draw a picture of what happens in it
exercise: Kai says, "This last text's well long, like." What helps? | Reading it in chunks and staying calm | Skipping the whole text and going home | Panicking first, then guessing at random | Reading only its title and nothing more
exercise: Ethan says, "This question is sus." What does "sus" mean? | Suspicious | Super | Silly | Simple
exercise: The teacher replies to Ethan, "Read it again." Why? | A calm second reading shows what is asked | Rereading earns an extra mark every time | The second reading turns the question easy | The first reading never counts in SATs
exercise: A child in Singapore asks, "Can skip or not?" Is it allowed to skip a question? | Yes, and come back if there's time | No, you must go strictly in order | Yes, but you can never come back to it | No, skipping counts as cheating
exercise: A mum in Glasgow says, "Dinnae panic, hen." What does she mean? | Don't panic, love | Don't eat, chicken | Stop it now, dear | Panic a bit, girl
exercise: Does panic help you focus in a test? | No; staying calm helps you think | Yes, panic sharpens the brain | Yes, but only in reading tests | No, but being sleepy does
exercise: Which answer uses evidence from the text? | He is brave because he "stepped into the dark cave alone". | He is brave because I think he is, so he is. | He is brave, obviously, and everyone knows it. | He is brave because brave people are always brave.
exercise: Are SATs questions designed to trick you? | No, but read them carefully | Yes, every question is a trap | Yes, so never trust any of them | No, so you can just skim them
test: Your mate says, "You gotta read every text super slow, three times, then answer." What is better? | Read each text once carefully, then answer | Read each text five times, very slowly | Answer without reading the texts at all | Read only the questions and never the texts
test: What would happen if you spent 40 of the 60 minutes on the first text? | Too little time would be left for the others | You would be given extra time at the end | The other two texts would become easier | Nothing, because time does not matter
test: A 3-mark question says, "Give three reasons why Sam was upset." Liam writes one long reason. What is most likely? | He gets one mark at most | He gets all three marks | He gets four marks | He gets two marks
test: "Find and copy one word that shows the house was old." The text says, "The crumbling house stood silent." What should you write? | crumbling | The crumbling house | silent | house stood
test: A cartoon says SATs decide whether you are allowed into secondary school. Fix it. | Everyone moves up; SATs mostly measure progress | Fail and you stay in Year 6 for another year | Only children with full marks get a place | SATs decide which of the sports teams you may join
test: Gran in Bristol: "Don't you go all teasy, my lover, 'tis only a test." ("Teasy" is West Country for cranky; "my lover" is a friendly greeting.) What does she mean? | Don't get grumpy, love, it's only a test | You are in love with the test, my dear | Go and tease your brother after the test | The test is very teasy and very hard
test: The text says, "Amira's hands shook as she knocked." The question: "How did Amira feel? Give evidence." What is the best answer? | Nervous: "her hands shook" | Happy: she knocked on a door | Angry: she knocked very hard | Sleepy: she stood at a door
test: Josh, rough: "Bloody hell, I ticked three on a 'tick two', I'm done for." What is true? | He loses that one mark, not the whole test | He fails the whole of the SATs now | He gets an extra mark for trying | The marker will just pick his best two
test: You meet a really hard question near the start. What is the best move? | Skip it, do the rest, then come back | Stare at it until the time runs out | Put your hand up and leave the room | Write a very long answer to it anyway
test: "Tick one: the main idea of the text is…" What should you look for? | What the whole text is mostly about | The longest word anywhere in the text | Only what the first sentence says | The funniest bit, wherever it is
test: "Which word in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to 'scared'?" Paragraph 2: "Ben was hungry and terrified, though he tried to look brave and cheerful." | terrified | brave | hungry | cheerful
exam: Identify the year group in England in which pupils customarily sit the national reading test known colloquially as SATs. | Year 6 | Year 2 | Year 9 | Year 11
exam: It is given that a question instructs candidates to "find and copy one word". Determine the most appropriate response. | Copy exactly one word from the text | Copy the entire relevant sentence | Paraphrase the passage at length | Supply a word of one's own choosing
exam: Ascertain the duration customarily allotted to the reading paper. | One hour | Fifteen minutes | Two hours | Three hours
exam: Select the response consistent with established guidance concerning questions requiring evidence. | Support the answer by reference to the text | Rely exclusively upon personal opinion | Omit all mention of the text whatsoever | Repeat the question verbatim as the answer
